B.Ip=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Spine 5",B.Tc)
B.Qd=s(["60 flexion, 50 extension","80 flexion, 70 extension","90 flexion, 90 extension","70 flexion, 80 extension"],t.s)
B.aa5=new A.e("What are the normal ranges of motion for wrist flexion and extension?",B.Qd,1,"Normal wrist flexion is approximately 80 degrees and extension is 70 degrees. There is slightly more flexion range than extension.")
B.W6=s(["Compartment 3; EPL","Compartment 1; APL and EPB (abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis)","Compartment 4; EDC and EIP","Compartment 6; ECU"],t.s)
B.aaR=new A.e("Which extensor compartment is the site of De Quervain's tenosynovitis, and what tendons does it contain?",B.W6,1,"De Quervain's involves the first extensor compartment containing the APL and EPB. Finkelstein's test (thumb tucked in fist with ulnar deviation) reproduces pain by stretching these tendons over the radial styloid.")
B.NW=s(["It has dual blood supply that can cause excessive bleeding","Blood enters distally and flows retrograde, so proximal fractures cut off blood supply causing avascular necrosis","It has no direct blood supply","Blood enters proximally and flows distally"],t.s)
B.ac6=new A.e("Why is the scaphoid fracture particularly dangerous regarding its blood supply?",B.NW,1,"The scaphoid's blood supply enters at the distal one-third and flows retrograde (backwards) to the proximal pole. A fracture at the waist or proximal pole cuts off blood supply to the proximal fragment, causing avascular necrosis.")
B.Mg=s(["50% aspiration, 25% surgical","90% aspiration recurrence, 10% surgical recurrence","30% aspiration, 5% surgical","70% aspiration, 30% surgical"],t.s)
B.aac=new A.e("What is the aspiration recurrence rate for ganglion cysts versus surgical excision?",B.Mg,1,"Aspiration has a 90% recurrence rate because it drains fluid but does not remove the stalk or valve mechanism. Surgical excision has only a 10% recurrence rate because it removes the entire cyst and stalk.")
B.OI=s(["Intrinsic muscle contracture pulling the extensor hood","Rupture of the central slip of the extensor tendon allowing the lateral bands to slip volarly, making them act as flexors at the PIP","Volar plate rupture at the DIP joint","Flexor tendon rupture at the MCP joint"],t.s)
B.a5u=new A.e("In RA of the hand, what mechanism causes a boutonniere deformity?",B.OI,1,"Boutonniere deformity occurs when the central slip of the extensor tendon ruptures. The lateral bands migrate volarly (below the axis of rotation) and become flexors at the PIP. The knuckle pops through like a button through a buttonhole (French: boutonniere).")
