B.a3n=new A.e("The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) primarily resists:",B.Xf,1,"The ACL prevents anterior translation of the tibia on the femur. It is tested with the Lachman test (most sensitive) and anterior drawer test. ACL tears are common in cutting/pivoting sports and have a female predominance (3-6x higher risk).")
B.VW=s(["It tests at full extension","At 20-30\xb0 flexion, the hamstrings are relaxed and can't mask laxity","It tests the posterior cruciate ligament simultaneously","It requires less examiner skill"],t.s)
B.a4K=new A.e("The Lachman test is considered more sensitive than the anterior drawer test for ACL tears because:",B.VW,1,"The Lachman test is performed at 20-30\xb0 flexion where hamstring guarding is minimized. The anterior drawer (90\xb0 flexion) allows the hamstrings to act as ACL synergists, potentially masking a tear. Lachman sensitivity is ~85-95% vs ~60-70% for anterior drawer.")
B.Pk=s(["ACL, LCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, MCL, and medial meniscus","PCL, MCL, and lateral meniscus","ACL, PCL, and medial meniscus"],t.s)
B.a3W=new A.e('The "unhappy triad" (O\'Donoghue triad) involves injury to which three structures?',B.Pk,1,"The classic unhappy triad: ACL tear + MCL tear + medial meniscus tear. It results from a valgus force with external rotation (e.g., a lateral blow to the knee). Note: some modern literature shows the lateral meniscus may be more commonly injured than medial.")
B.OP=s(["ACL","PCL","Meniscus","MCL"],t.s)
B.a6O=new A.e("The McMurray test evaluates which knee structure?",B.OP,2,"McMurray's test: with the knee flexed, apply valgus + external rotation (medial meniscus) or varus + internal rotation (lateral meniscus) while extending. A pop or click with pain = positive for meniscal tear.")
B.Qt=s(["Lachman test","Anterior drawer test","Posterior drawer test / posterior sag sign","Pivot shift test"],t.s)
B.a4C=new A.e("The PCL is best tested with which maneuver?",B.Qt,2,"The posterior drawer test: at 90\xb0 flexion, push the tibia posteriorly. PCL tears also show a posterior sag sign \u2014 with knees flexed at 90\xb0, the tibial plateau sags posteriorly compared to the uninjured side. Dashboard injuries (tibia pushed back) are the classic mechanism.")
B.Ou=s([B.a3n,B.a4K,B.a3W,B.a6O,B.a4C],t.b)
B.IQ=new A.a6("Musculoskeletal: Knee 1",B.Ou)
B.Mk=s(["At Guyon's canal","5-8 cm proximal to the wrist","At the cubital tunnel","At the axilla"],t.s)
B.aaj=new A.e("The dorsal ulnar cutaneous (DUC) nerve branches from the ulnar nerve at what location?",B.Mk,1,"The DUC branches 5-8 cm proximal to the wrist and does NOT travel through Guyon's canal. This is a critical landmark: DUC abnormal = lesion at or above the elbow. DUC normal = lesion at wrist (Guyon's canal).")
B.Un=s(["First dorsal interosseous","Adductor pollicis","Opponens pollicis","Flexor pollicis longus"],t.s)
B.a3I=new A.e("Froment's sign tests for weakness of which muscle?",B.Un,1,"Froment's sign: patient tries to hold paper between thumb and index finger. The adductor pollicis (ulnar nerve) is weak, so the patient compensates by flexing the thumb IP joint using FPL (median nerve). IP flexion during pinch = positive Froment sign = ulnar neuropathy.")
B.WH=s(["Radiation causes fasciculations; tumor causes fibrillations","Radiation shows myokymic discharges; tumor is typically painful with Horner syndrome","They cannot be differentiated on EMG","Radiation affects lower trunk; tumor affects upper trunk"],t.s)
B.abH=new A.e("How do you differentiate radiation-induced brachial plexopathy from tumor recurrence on EMG?",B.WH,1,"Key differentiators: Radiation plexopathy = painless, upper trunk predominant, myokymic discharges (marching soldiers on EMG). Tumor recurrence = painful, lower trunk predominant, may have Horner syndrome (ptosis, miosis, anhydrosis). Myokymia is the EMG key.")
B.Pg=s(["Thumb and index finger","Index and middle fingers","Ring and little fingers","All five digits equally"],t.s)
B.a6S=new A.e("Ulnar claw hand affects primarily which digits?",B.Pg,2,"Ulnar claw hand primarily affects the 4th and 5th digits. The unopposed EDC hyperextends the MCPs, and passive tension on the flexor tendons curls the PIP/DIP joints. The index and middle fingers are relatively spared because their lumbricals are median-innervated.")
B.Mb=s(["Inability to cross the fingers","The small finger drifting into abduction at rest due to weak interossei","Weakness of thumb opposition","Inability to extend the wrist"],t.s)
B.a6f=new A.e("Wartenberg's sign is demonstrated by:",B.Mb,1,"Wartenberg's sign: the small finger drifts into abduction because the third palmar interosseous (ulnar nerve) is weak and cannot adduct the 5th digit against the unopposed action of EDM. It is a subtle early sign of ulnar neuropathy.")
