B.a6P=new A.e("For tendon transfer surgery in SCI, what is the minimum muscle grade required for a donor muscle?",B.Pq,1,"A donor muscle must be grade 4 or ideally grade 5 because the muscle loses one full grade of strength during the transfer process. A grade 3 muscle would become grade 2 (non-functional against gravity) after transfer.")
B.Mw=s(["Deltoid to triceps","Brachioradialis to ECRB (extensor carpi radialis brevis)","Pronator teres to ECRL","FDS to EDC"],t.s)
B.abF=new A.e("What is the classic tendon transfer for a C5-level tetraplegic to restore wrist extension?",B.Mw,1,"At C5 level, the brachioradialis (an elbow flexor made redundant by the intact biceps) is transferred to the ECRB to restore active wrist extension, enabling tenodesis grasp.")
B.Ug=s(["The patient has normal two-point discrimination","The patient requires visual feedback to distinguish objects and cannot feel objects smaller than 10mm with eyes closed","The patient is completely insensate","The patient has intact light touch only"],t.s)
B.a94=new A.e("In the upper limb classification for tendon transfers, what does the O (ocular) designation indicate about the patient's sensation?",B.Ug,1,"O (ocular) means the patient requires visual feedback to distinguish objects. They cannot tell what they are holding if their eyes are closed for items smaller than 10mm. Q (cutaneous) indicates better sensation with two-point discrimination <10mm.")
B.Wu=s(["Moberg key grip procedure","Brachioradialis to FPL transfer (BR to FPL)","Deltoid to triceps transfer","Zancolli lasso procedure"],t.s)
B.aar=new A.e("What surgical procedure restores active lateral key grip at the C6 level by powering the thumb flexor?",B.Wu,1,"The BR to FPL (brachioradialis to flexor pollicis longus) transfer provides an active lateral pinch at C6 level. Many surgeons prefer this over the Moberg procedure because it creates an active rather than static grip.")
B.Zb=s(["The arm is stuck in pronation; release the pronator teres","The biceps is unopposed as a supinator causing supination contracture; reroute the biceps tendon around the radial neck","The triceps overpowers the biceps; lengthen the triceps","Wrist drop from radial nerve palsy; tendon transfer"],t.s)
B.aaJ=new A.e("What supination problem occurs in C5-C6 injuries and what is the surgical correction?",B.Zb,1,"In C5-C6 injuries, the biceps is an unopposed powerful supinator (no working pronator to counteract it), causing the arm to get stuck in supination. The biceps tendon can be rerouted around the radial neck to correct this.")
