B.Rm=s(["Administer benzodiazepines","Apply physical restraints","Assess the environment and ensure safety through environmental modifications","Start maintenance medications such as SSRIs"],t.s)
B.a5V=new A.e("What is the first step in managing an agitated patient with TBI according to the hierarchy of intervention?",B.Rm,2,"The source material emphasizes that the first step is not medication. You begin with environmental modifications such as floor beds, net beds, and safe rooms to allow agitation to occur without risk of injury.")
B.X0=s(["It sedates the patient through dopamine depletion","It improves frontal lobe attention and processing speed, reducing confusion-driven agitation","It acts as a long-term anxiolytic","It directly blocks sympathetic overdrive"],t.s)
B.abX=new A.e("Why might a neurostimulant such as methylphenidate paradoxically reduce agitation in a TBI patient?",B.X0,1,"Agitation often results from profound confusion due to poor attention and slow processing. A neurostimulant can help the frontal lobes function better, allowing the patient to understand their environment and reducing agitation.")
B.PK=s(["50% to 75% of expected metabolic needs","75% to 100% of expected metabolic needs","100% to 140% of expected metabolic needs","140% to 200% of expected metabolic needs"],t.s)
B.a8l=new A.e("What is the recommended caloric replacement level for a patient in the acute phase of TBI due to the hypermetabolic state?",B.PK,2,"Acute TBI triggers a massive hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic state, with energy requirements jumping to 100% to 140% of expected levels due to catecholamine release, cortisol, and increased muscle tone.")
B.YK=s(["Methylphenidate","Propranolol","Trazodone","Valproic acid"],t.s)
B.a53=new A.e("Which medication is specifically recommended for sympathetic storming (tachycardia, hypertension, diaphoresis) in TBI patients?",B.YK,1,"Propranolol, a beta blocker, is highlighted as a maintenance medication that is effective when there is a sympathetic overdrive component such as storming with tachycardia, hypertension, and diaphoresis.")
B.TN=s(["Sundowning","Urinary tract infection","Iatrogenic withdrawal from sedatives such as fentanyl or midazolam","Undiagnosed seizure activity"],t.s)
B.a69=new A.e("When evaluating an agitated TBI patient, which of the following is described as an often-missed cause of agitation related to medications given in the ICU?",B.TN,2,"The transcript specifically highlights that iatrogenic withdrawal is frequently missed. Patients who received fentanyl and midazolam drips in the ICU for weeks and then had them abruptly discontinued may experience withdrawal from those same medications.")
